{"name":"Bunting Blue","hex":"#35537c","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"bunting","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Strips of material used as festive decoration, especially in the colours of the national flag.","A thin cloth of woven wool from which flags are made; it is light enough to spread in a gentle wind but resistant to fraying in a strong wind."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Any of various songbirds of the genus Emberiza, having short bills and brown or gray plumage."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/bunting"},{"term":"blue","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a blue hue.","Depressed, melancholic, sad."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The colour of the clear sky or the deep sea; the colour midway between green and violet in the visible spectrum and one of the primary additive colours.","Anything coloured blue, especially to distinguish it from similar objects differing only in colour."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/blue"}],"entries":[{"title":"Bunting (decoration)","blocks":[{"text":"Bunting refers to decorative flags, wide streamers, or draperies made of fabric, or of plastic, paper or cardboard in imitation of fabric. Bunting is also a collection of flags, and the fabric used to make flags. The fabric was originally a specific type of lightweight worsted wool fabric, but can also be cotton."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d1/Buntingprofessionals.JPG/330px-Buntingprofessionals.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bunting_(decoration)"},{"title":"Blue","blocks":[{"text":"Blue is one of the three primary colours in the RGB (additive) colour model, as well as in the RYB colour model. It lies between violet and cyan on the spectrum of visible light. The term blue generally describes colours perceived by humans observing light with a dominant wavelength between approximately 450 and 495 nanometres. The clear daytime sky and the deep sea appear blue because of an optical effect known as Rayleigh scattering. An optical effect called the Tyndall effect explains blue eyes. Distant objects appear more blue because of another optical effect called aerial perspective."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg/330px-Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blue"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2c/June_19th_%22Bunting%22.jpg/500px-June_19th_%22Bunting%22.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2c/June_19th_%22Bunting%22.jpg/1280px-June_19th_%22Bunting%22.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2c/June_19th_%22Bunting%22.jpg/500px-June_19th_%22Bunting%22.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Bunting in the form of triangular flags in the West Midlands, United Kingdom","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AJune_19th_%22Bunting%22.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/49/2004_-_4th_of_July_-_Roche_Harbor_019.jpg/250px-2004_-_4th_of_July_-_Roche_Harbor_019.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/49/2004_-_4th_of_July_-_Roche_Harbor_019.jpg/500px-2004_-_4th_of_July_-_Roche_Harbor_019.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/49/2004_-_4th_of_July_-_Roche_Harbor_019.jpg/250px-2004_-_4th_of_July_-_Roche_Harbor_019.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thu